## What runs today (measured 2026-09-09, aarch64-macos, amu --jvm-free)

Reproduction, not values -- the values move:

    A=orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/bin/amu
    printf '(ns t (:export [main]))\\n(defn main [] :i64 ...)\\n' > /tmp/t.kotoba
    $A compile /tmp/t.kotoba --jvm-free --target aarch64-macos --output /tmp/t.kexe
    $A extract-native /tmp/t.kexe --symbol main --output /tmp/m.bin
    cc -O2 -o /tmp/loader orgs/kotoba-lang/amu/tools/kexe_loader.c
    /tmp/loader /tmp/m.bin <offset> <arity> aarch64 <allow-csv|->

| face | state |
|---|---|
| .kotoba to machine code, executed | works |
| fs/browse (wire 34), directory listing, executed | works |
| fs/app-data (wire 35) read / write / ranged read | hosted by the loader |
| env/read (wire 33) | hosted by the loader |
| string-substring / string-byte-length / string-contains? | lower to native |
| string-index-of | REFUSED, see below |
| stdout, argv, exit status | do not exist as capabilities at all |
| standalone executable | does not exist |

The last two rows are what stands between this and a command. The loader is a
HOST: it mmaps the code, answers capability calls, and prints the RETURN VALUE
as an EDN map. There is no way for a guest to write a byte to stdout, read its
own argv, or set an exit status, and `kotoba.native.macho` encodes a `.o`
object, not a linked executable. `capability-catalog.edn` lists 35 capabilities
and none of them is stdout or argv -- so this is not a qualification gap in a
kit, it is an absent capability.

## The first blocker, and why it was the right one to take first

`string-index-of` failed as `aggregate ABI rejected: call-abi-not-admitted`.
Without an offset a guest cannot cut a string at a separator, so it cannot
split lines -- which removes grep, wc, cut, head, tail, sort and uniq at once,
and is why `kbb.browse` could not compile for native even though the loader has
hosted its provider all along.

It turned out to be one line of exposure, not a feature: `kotoba$string-find`,
the helper kotoba-native ADR 0002 already had, ALREADY answers the first
matching byte offset. `string-contains?` was folding that offset to 0/1 and
throwing it away.

Landed as three changes, because there are three gates in front of it and ADR
0002 measured that opening one alone moves nothing:

  * kotoba-native `lower-index-of` + dispatch on both ISAs (its ADR 0081)
  * kotoba-verifier `string-operations` gains `string-index-of 2` (its ADR 0051)
  * osaho: the oracle itself was wrong (its ADR 0271)

## The reference interpreter was the implementation that was wrong

kotoba-native's string-search suite never writes an expected value down: each
row runs through `kotoba.kir/execute` twice, once as the operation and once as
the rewrite. Adding an astral row made that suite fail as `(not (= 7 4))` on
`(string-index-of` G-clef + `ab, ab)`.

`kotoba.kir.value/utf8-index-of!` walked the haystack one UTF-16 unit at a
time, charged a high surrogate 4 bytes, then stepped onto the LOW surrogate and
charged 3 more. Every astral code point before a match added 7. The JS emitter,
the new native lowering and CPython all answer 4.

Nothing had caught it because every multi-byte test row used 2- and 3-byte code
points, which are one UTF-16 unit each. The existing test even named the wrong
answer it was guarding against -- a UTF-16 answer of 6 -- and 7 is neither that
nor the right one. It also reached artifacts, not just runtime: `lower` folds a
pure i64 entry through this evaluator, so a constant `string-index-of` over an
astral literal was baked in wrong.

This is the workspace rule about oracles paying for itself. A suite that had
written 4 down would have been GREEN and the reference would still be wrong.

## Decisions

1. Foundation before repos, each step proved by EXECUTING the artifact rather
   than by building it. The remaining foundation is: argv, stdout/stderr and
   exit status as capabilities with loader providers, then a linked executable
   so `./ls` is a file rather than a blob plus a loader invocation.
2. Naming is the ORIGIN plane. POSIX utilities are specified by IEEE Std
   1003.1, so they take `org-ieee-<utility>`, following `org-ieee-tar`, which
   states that pattern for exactly this reason. Non-POSIX entries in the
   owner's list route to their own origin instead -- ssh is IETF and
   `org-ietf-ssh` already exists.
3. The command set is the 28 the owner listed. `tar` is already
   `org-ieee-tar` (a format library, not yet a command surface); awk, sed,
   curl and ssh are each a larger subject than the rest and are sequenced last.

## Not done, and named so it is not mistaken for done

No command repo exists yet, and none can produce a runnable binary until argv,
stdout and exit status exist. Do not scaffold 28 repos against a runtime that
cannot print. The next step is the loader's provider table, not a repo.
"}]
